    },
    {
      "id": "checksum",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "Checksum",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 2,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": [
        "RFC 9293 §3.1: 'the 16-bit ones' complement of the ones' complement sum of all 16-bit words in the",
        "header and text', computed over a pseudo-header prefixed to the segment.",
        "",
        "The pseudo-header is a set here (see pseudoHeader) rather than something the checksum converter",
        "knows about privately. It is laid out and then read as input, which keeps the fact that TCP's",
        "checksum reaches outside its own header a visible part of the description."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "urgentPointer",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "Urgent Pointer",
      "form": {
        "of": "scalar",
        "octets": 2,
        "big": true,
        "signed": false
      },
      "about": "RFC 9293 §3.1: 'only to be interpreted in segments with the URG control bit set.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "options",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "Options",
      "about": [
        "RFC 9293 §3.1: 'Options may occupy space at the end of the TCP header and are a multiple of 8 bits in",
        "length.' How many there are is nowhere in the segment — Data Offset says where the header ends and",
        "the options are whatever fills the space, which is a fact about a segment rather than about TCP.",
        "",
        "So going out this set is written once per item of the list it requires, and coming in the reading goes",
        "round until the header ends. Neither direction has a count and neither needs one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "data",
      "kind": "field",
      "as": "Data",
      "form": {
        "of": "opaque"
      },
      "about": "Everything after the header. Its extent is what the carrier says, less the header."
    },
    {
