    {
      "id": "evaluated.optionsAsked",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "did the caller ask for options?",
      "runs": "state.synchronising == 1",
      "about": [
        "What gates the options on the way out. It points at a state, not at the SYN flag:",
        "whether options may be carried is a fact about where the connection has got to, and",
        "reading the flag instead would make the arrangement depend on a field of the very",
        "message it is arranging."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "state.synchronising",
      "kind": "state",
      "as": "Synchronising",
      "purpose": "Whether this connection is still being established. RFC 9293 §3.1 allows the Maximum Segment Size option only on segments with SYN set, which is a fact about where the connection has got to rather than about this segment."
    },
    {
      "id": "parsed",
      "kind": "end-parse",
      "as": "end of the segment",
      "about": [
        "Where reading a segment is allowed to stop. Everything before the options is fixed, so the only way a",
        "reading ends anywhere else is that the option run went wrong — which is precisely the case that would",
        "otherwise report success having read a prefix."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "evaluated.moreOptions",
      "kind": "evaluated",
      "label": "is there another option?",
      "runs": "position < fields.dataOffset.value * 4 && fields.optionKind.value != 0",
      "about": [
        "Two ways the option run ends, and RFC 9293 §3.1 gives both. It ends where the header ends,",
        "which Data Offset says; and it ends at an End of Option List, after which 'the content of the",
        "header ... MUST be header padding of zeros'.",
        "",
        "Without the second, the padding reads as more options — which is not even wrong, since a zero",
        "octet IS kind 0. It is just that everything after the first one is padding rather than a run of",
        "them."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "option",
      "kind": "set",
      "as": "an option",
      "about": [
        "RFC 9293 §3.1 gives two cases: a single octet of option-kind, or option-kind followed by",
        "option-length and that many octets in total. Which applies is the KIND, and the split is at two —",
        "so this describes the shapes rather than the kinds, and an option this file has never heard of is",
        "read and written correctly by falling into the second one."
      ]
    },
